// Components and relationships of the semantic core: validation, the model, transactions, layers and derivation.
{
  // ---- Components ------------------------------------------------------------------
  "components": [
    // ======== Subsystems ==============================================================
    {
      "id": "semantic-core",
      "name": "Semantic core",
      "kind": "subsystem",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "Owns the canonical semantic model and every rule about it; runs unchanged in the browser and in Node.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Derive the model from specification text and validate it",
        "Apply typed operations atomically as source-preserving text edits",
        "Evaluate claims and simulate state machines on the model"
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "src/core/types.js"
        ],
        "notes": "types.js holds JSDoc typedefs only. The directory src/core also contains projections.js, layout.js with the modules under layout/, and generate/, which this file classifies under visualization and tooling by responsibility, and own.js, claimed by queries; they import nothing outside src/core.",
        "language": "mixed"
      },
      "parent": "exegra"
    },
    // ======== Semantic core =============================================================
    {
      "id": "metamodel",
      "name": "Meta-model",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "storage",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "owner": "semantic-core",
      "purpose": "Describes entity kinds, their properties, the built-in categories and relationship kinds and the top-level sections of a specification as data.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Define which properties each entity kind accepts and their types",
        "Define the built-in vocabulary (categories, relationship kinds, shapes, colours, projections) that a specification may extend",
        "Define the id pattern and the schema version (0.1)",
        "Define the claim kinds (structural, invariant, temporal, measured, requirement) and the view definition: filter (categories, relationship kinds, within, tags, entity kinds) and display (ports, colour by, reference depth, layout method)",
        "Define the record and copy kinds of the data model: field types, personal-data classes, roles, copy scopes (shared, per-session, per-principal, internal) and the units of freshness and erasure bounds"
      ],
      "unknowns": ["The built-in entity kinds, claim kinds, claim evaluation methods and projections are fixed per build. A specification can add entity kinds, properties of any kind, categories, relationship kinds and verification methods (carried out outside Exegra) through its vocabulary, but it cannot add an evaluation method that Exegra itself runs."],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "src/core/metamodel.json"
        ],
        "notes": "Hand-written data file.",
        "language": "json"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "claim-method-registry",
      "name": "Claim method registry",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "storage",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "owner": "semantic-core",
      "purpose": "Declares the supported claim evaluation methods with their parameters, and the five claim statuses.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "List each method, the claim kinds it can evaluate and its typed parameters",
        "Define the claim status labels and tones that every consumer shows",
        "Declare `negatives` as a parameter of every trace method (state-invariant, precedes, eventually, never): a list of {scenario, faults}, each one of the claim's scenarios run with extra faults",
        "Declare the refines method: one implementation behavior and optionally the scenarios whose traces are checked"
      ],
      "unknowns": ["The registry declares methods but the evaluator implements them separately; adding an entry without code leaves the claim not evaluated."],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "src/core/claim-methods.js"
        ],
        "notes": "Hand-written data module used by the parser, validator, evaluator and inspector.",
        "language": "js"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "parser",
      "name": "Parser",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "The text layer of the specification: parses the JSONC source (comments, trailing commas, syntax errors with locations), hands the document to the model derivation in the Lean kernel and maps the raw paths it reports to lines and columns.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Parse JSONC with comments and trailing commas, reporting syntax errors with locations",
        "Encode the document for the kernel with every object's members in source order and repeated keys kept, and pass the meta-model and the claim method registry as data",
        "Map every raw path and part the kernel reports (node, key of a member, first character) to an offset, length, line and column, and put the line of the earlier declaration into an id.duplicate message",
        "Rebuild the model the rest of the shell reads (entities by id and kind, references, expressions, effective meta-model, vocabulary, semantic hash) from the kernel's reply, with prototype-free data objects as jsonc-parser builds them"
      ],
      "unknowns": [
        "The whole text is re-parsed on every commit and on every source-editor analysis; there is no incremental parsing. Measured 2026-11-18 in Node 24 on Windows 10 (wasm64, median of 10 warm runs, parse and hash): specs/exegra (788 KB) 243 ms through the Lean kernel against 35 ms for the JavaScript it replaced; specs/benchmark (134 KB) 67 ms against 9 ms. Of the 243 ms about 95 ms is Exegra.Json.parse reading the request (WG-4797), 27 ms the derivation, 10 ms reference resolution, 33 ms the hash and 45 ms writing the reply. In Chromium (Playwright, tests/browser/lean-core.spec.js) the same parse and hash take 312 ms (median of 7).",
        "The schema.version error message tells the user to run an `exegra migrate` command that does not exist."
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "language": "js",
        "modules": [
          "src/core/parse.js"
        ],
        "notes": "Outside the verdict path: it only parses text and maps locations; the model is derived by model-derivation in Lean."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "validator",
      "name": "Validator",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "Applies whole-model semantic rules to a parsed model and reports errors, warnings and specification gaps; analyzeSpec composes parser and validator.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Check containment, behaviors, transitions, effects, faults, scenarios, claim parameters and views",
        "Accept an evaluation method the specification declares as a verification method (it takes no parameters), and report any other method this build does not run as unsupported",
        "Check every expression's references against the scope it is used in",
        "Report gaps (missing purpose, unconnected component, unused event, claim that can only rest on external evidence and has none) as info diagnostics; a requirement claim is not reported for lacking evidence",
        "Provide analyzeSpec: parse, then validate only when the structure is sound",
        "Check events that name a contract: a role is required, the payload fields are among the inputs (request) or outputs (response), every input that is not optional is present, an error carries a code among the contract's errors, and declared field types are assignable to the contract's; an unknown contract is a reference error",
        "Run the data model's consistency checks (record keys and copy fields name declared fields, bounds have units) as errors and its six data rules as warnings, each naming the record or copy and the field",
        "Check each negative of a trace claim: its scenario is one of the claim's scenarios, and its faults are a non-empty list of declared faults none of which that scenario already activates",
        "Report integrity rules: an invalid rule as an error, a vacuous rule as a warning, and each violation with the rule severity (integrity.violated), left out of analyzeSpec's blocking error count",
        "Check refinement declarations: only an implementation-level behavior refines, the refined behavior does not refine in turn, refines comes with a refinement mapping, the mapping names only states and transitions of the right sides and variables of the model, its expressions read only the implementation's variables, and every implementation state and transition and every model variable is mapped; a refines claim names one refining behavior and only scenarios that run it",
        "Check timer delays read at run time ({expr, simulate}): only on an implementation-level machine, expr an expression of a number over the machine variables, time and the event payload, and simulate the delay a simulated run uses (simulationInputV2 lowers the timer with it)",
        "Check time and integers in implementation-level machines (src/core/readiness-time.js, WG-4902): a variable compared with time holds a value set from time wherever it is read (ready.time-epoch); time - x is read only after x <= time (ready.time-difference); the scenarios running the machine agree on a time unit, fixed delays are whole milliseconds and a machine reading time runs in ms (ready.delay-unit); literals, integer initial values and scenario payload values are within their integer range, at most 2^53 - 1 (ready.number-bound)",
        "Check the realisation and durability of machines (src/core/realisation.js, WG-4922): behavior.realisation and contract.deployment (shipped or target, defaulting from the component's implementation status; an explicit shipped on a not-implemented component is behavior.realisation-subject or contract.deployment-subject) and storage.durability (record or memory; a memory machine names no record, creates none and declares no transaction, or storage.durability)",
        "Check residency (src/core/residency.js): follow each record a tenant class binds to its owner, the holder of each copy and both ends of each relationship that carries it, and report a component that cannot keep a tenant of the class in its permitted regions (residency.record-outside, residency.copy-outside, residency.flow-outside) unless an active residency exception names that copy or flow; a holder of bound data states its placement (residency.placement-missing) and a per-region one has an instance in every provisioned region (residency.region-uncovered); placement and regions agree (component.placement-regions); a tenant class has one selector and at least one region (tenant-class.selector, tenant-class.provisioned-in); an exception names one copy or flow of bound data, its durations are durations, a bounded one states its purge and a renewal window is shorter than its longest duration (residency.exception-target, residency.exception-duration, residency.exception-purge, residency.exception-renewal)",
        "Check quota pools (src/core/pool-rules.js): at least one pooled limit with a unique lower-case name (pool.limits), exactly one of quota and a positive perMember (pool.limit-contribution), a pooled quota counted per tenant over the pool's window as a rate (pool.quota-scope, pool.quota-window, pool.limit-kind) and held by the pool's component (pool.held-by), usage and exemption events with the payload fields the pool reads (pool.usage-event, pool.event-payload), the window, over-share count and release (pool.window, pool.over-share, pool.release), 1 to 4 modelled members (pool.model-members), an exemption role that confers its capability (pool.exemption-capability) and machine ids no entity takes (pool.machine-id); a scenario runs a behavior or a pool (scenario.empty), and a claim expression reads only variables of the machines of pools every listed scenario runs (expr.reference)"
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "src/core/validate.js",
          "src/core/analyze.js",
          "src/core/evidence-ref.js",
          "src/core/readiness-machines.js",
          "src/core/readiness-time.js",
          "src/core/realisation.js",
          "src/core/residency.js",
          "src/core/pool-rules.js"
        ],
        "notes": "The rules are hard-coded. Claim parameter checks read the claim method registry, the gap rules read `expected` flags of the effective meta-model and view filters are checked against the effective vocabulary.",
        "language": "js"
      },
      "unknowns": [
        "A residency exception's expiry, continuous renewal and purge are recorded and shape-checked but not executed, so claims about them are not decided by exploration (WG-5282)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "expression-language",
      "name": "Expression language",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "A small side-effect-free expression language for guards, effect values and claim expressions; no host-language evaluation is involved.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Tokenize and parse expressions (implication, boolean and arithmetic operators, comparisons, string and number literals, dotted references with hyphenated ids)",
        "Evaluate an expression against a caller-supplied reference resolver",
        "List the references an expression uses so the validator can check scope",
        "Evaluate with the declared types of references when the caller gives them (WG-4902): checked integer arithmetic in u8, u16, u32, i32 and in u64 and i64 limited to +-(2^53 - 1), integer division truncating toward zero and remainder with the sign of the dividend, an absent option read as null; structural equality of lists and objects always; the same rules as eval in formal/Exegra/Expr.lean and the generated code (docs/semantics.md, Values, numbers and time)"
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "src/core/expr.js"
        ],
        "notes": "The validator and the claim evaluator use src/core/expr.js. The simulation engine has its own implementation of the same language in Rust (engine/exegra-engine/src/expr.rs), held to the same parse and evaluation semantics and error messages by the conformance goldens.",
        "language": "js"
      },
      "tags": [
        "verdict-kernel"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "model-hash",
      "name": "Normal form and hash",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "Produces the deterministic normal form of a model (independent of comments, whitespace, key order, top-level declaration order and source positions) and its 53-bit semantic hash, in the verified kernel.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Normal form: schema version, vocabulary and the top-level declarations sorted by id with every object's keys sorted as JavaScript sorts strings (UTF-16 code units)",
        "Semantic hash: cyrb53 over the UTF-16 code units of the normal form's JSON text, bit-identical to the JavaScript it replaced; used for revision identity, optimistic concurrency of transactions and claim results",
        "Prove that the hash depends only on semantic content: declaration order and presentation (Exegra.Model.Model.semanticHash_presentation) and key order at every depth (Exegra.Hash.semanticHash_key_order); what runs is the definition (Exegra.Hash.semanticHash'_eq)"
      ],
      "unknowns": [
        "The hash is not cryptographic (cyrb53); it identifies revisions and detects change, it does not resist collisions.",
        "The key-order theorem assumes distinct keys in every object; the derivation produces such values (it assigns members like JavaScript), but that is not itself stated as a theorem yet."
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "language": "lean",
        "modules": [
          "formal/Exegra/Hash.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/HashProps.lean"
        ],
        "trusts": [
          "trusted-lean-kernel",
          "trusted-lean-compiler-runtime",
          "trusted-emscripten-toolchain",
          "trusted-libuv",
          "trusted-js-glue",
          "trusted-wasm-engine"
        ],
        "notes": "The Lean kernel of the hash only. The JavaScript that calls it is hash-binding (src/core/hash.js), a separate component, so this component's dependencies are only the Lean definitions it uses. Stable JSON output and SHA-256 are stable-serialization."
      },
      "tags": [
        "verdict-kernel"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "queries",
      "name": "Model queries",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "Answers containment, dependency, selection, reference and contract questions about a model for every consumer.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Resolve ports to components, list children, ancestors, descendants and owned entities",
        "Build the directed component graph and traverse dependencies",
        "Select components by id, category, kind, tag, trust zone or containment",
        "Find the declaration site of an entity kind",
        "Provide own(), the own-property lookup that the parser, validator, claim evaluator, queries and Project use for every spec-supplied key"
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "src/core/query.js",
          "src/core/own.js",
          "src/core/interactions.js"
        ],
        "notes": "own.js is a one-function helper; it is claimed here because the lookup it provides is imported by the other core modules that read spec-supplied keys.",
        "language": "js"
      },
      "tags": [
        "verdict-kernel"
      ],
      "unknowns": [
        "Hierarchies, cuts and facets are declared and queried (cutMap, blockOf, cutToCut, facetMembers, knowledgeOf); quotients over cuts and level-aware claims use them (semantic-abstraction), but views that adopt a declared cut are WG-5695.",
        "Module elements are derived from the declared implementation.modules, not extracted from the code, so the module-to-element map is only as true as those declarations (WG-5696).",
        "Hierarchy, cut and facet well-formedness is checked in JavaScript (validate.js and query.js); the Lean derivation accepts the sections as data but does not check them, and laminar families and cut maps have no Lean definitions or theorems yet (WG-5699)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "transactions",
      "name": "Transaction system",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "Applies typed operations atomically as source-preserving text edits, with revisions, undo, redo, reload and commit notification; it is the only place where a project's state changes.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Check operation preconditions and compute JSONC edits that keep comments and formatting",
        "Reject any transaction whose resulting specification has validation errors (violations of integrity rules excepted) or whose layout would be unreadable; nothing changes on rejection",
        "Keep presentation edits (layout) out of the specification text",
        "Record history sufficient for undo and redo and increment a revision on every commit",
        "Adopt text that changed outside the process (reload): text with syntax errors is refused, any text that parses is adopted, including text with validation errors; the adoption clears the undo and redo stacks and is itself not undoable",
        "Leave the model and its semantic hash as they were when a commit changes only presentation text",
        "Notify subscribers synchronously after each commit",
        "Reject a transaction that adds a violation of an integrity rule of severity error that the project did not have before (the ratchet), with the counterexamples; existing violations do not block unrelated edits",
        "Re-derive the model once per transaction, not once per operation: keep an index of where every entity is declared that follows creations, property edits, removals, replacements of nested declarations and id changes, re-derive only for an operation that reads references, expressions or entity data (deletion, rename, source replacement) or after an edit the index cannot follow, and splice the edits of each declaration's lines into the text once; the resulting text, failures and verdict are those of re-deriving after every edit, and a transaction whose index is found not to match its model is replayed that way"
      ],
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "transactions-apply-in",
          "name": "Apply transaction",
          "direction": "provides",
          "contract": "apply-transaction",
          "protocol": "in-process call (Project.apply)",
          "description": "Submit one operation or an array of operations as one transaction."
        }
      ],
      "unknowns": [
        "History, undo and redo stacks are unbounded in memory and are not persisted: undo is lost on reload and per CLI invocation.",
        "Only operation transactions and replacement sources are validated before they commit. Undo and redo restore text that was committed before and do not validate again, and reload commits any text that parses, so the model after a reload can carry validation errors.",
        "The session never passes expectRevision or expectHash; only the CLI uses the optimistic checks (--expect-hash).",
        "A deletion or a rename re-derives the model of the current text before it plans its edits, because references and expressions of entities edited earlier in the transaction are known only to a derivation: about 2.6 s each on a 7 MB specification (WG-4980).",
        "The validation of a transaction's result is a full validation: rule and example results are not cached per entity (WG-4974). Measured on a 7 MB specification, validation is about 0.9 s of a single-property apply; the model derivation is about 2.6 s."
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "src/core/operations.js",
          "src/core/project.js",
          "src/core/text-edit.js"
        ],
        "notes": "The operation table is hand-written; CreateEntity looks up declaration sites in the meta-model. Tx keeps the index of declarations, the windows over edited declarations and the pending appends; Project.apply's perOperation option runs the reference path that re-derives after every edit.",
        "language": "js"
      },
      "tags": [
        "verdict-kernel"
      ],
      "interface": {
        "inputs": [
          "edit-applied",
          "model-parsed",
          "node-moved",
          "operation-submitted",
          "project-opened",
          "redo-requested",
          "reload-requested",
          "undo-requested",
          "validation-failed",
          "validation-passed"
        ],
        "outputs": [
          "commit-notified",
          "text-edits-computed",
          "transaction-committed",
          "transaction-rejected"
        ],
        "observables": [
          "transaction-flow.revision"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "claim-evaluator",
      "name": "Claim evaluator",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "Evaluates claims against a model and recorded evidence results, reporting a status, scope and counterexample that never overstate what was checked.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Evaluate structural claims (no-relationship, reachable, only-via, has-port, property-defined, acyclic) on the component graph; report a selector that selects nothing as inconclusive, never verified; reachable needs a path of at least one relationship (a target that is also a source does not count); only-via is inconclusive when no directed path of the kinds leads from any source to any target at all, and when every source is itself a via component (both hold by construction, not by the model)",
        "Evaluate invariant and temporal claims on traces of the listed scenarios (state-invariant, precedes, eventually, never), stating that only those traces were checked; never report a vacuous trace check as verified: an implication whose antecedent is never true, a never whose event cannot occur, a precedes whose then-event never happens and traces with zero steps are inconclusive; an eventually whose occurrence is only the scenario's own scripted input is inconclusive, because the input is not a reaction of the system",
        "Turn every check it runs over the model, every recorded evidence result, theorem check, external outcome and assumption fact into a fact in the Lean schema, encode each claim as a part or a conjunction of parts (one per piece of test evidence for an evidence-based claim), and take every status from the Lean verdict; it words the result and decides no status",
        "Pass each recorded fact with the stamp it was recorded on and the current stamp the caller gives (tools/tree-stamp.js); the freshness of each fact and its effect on the status are decided by Lean: a stale positive fact never makes a claim positive (an explanation starting with Stale and the diagnostic claim.stale-evidence or claim.stale-theorem), a stale contradicting fact still counts, and without current stamps a recorded fact is unverifiable and establishes nothing (diagnostic claim.freshness-unverifiable)",
        "Hand each recorded Lean theorem check to the Lean verdict with the claim's about constants, and word a refusal from the reason Lean gives",
        "Report a claim whose method is a verification method declared by the specification with evaluator external:<method>: Exegra runs nothing and the status comes only from evidence",
        "Check a trace step by step for the simulator panel (checkTrace)",
        "Add the automatic contract-conformance checks of a trace to the checks of checkTrace (src/core/contract-trace.js), as failed checks with the step",
        "Evaluate data-model claims (method data-model, a list of rules) on the records and copies of the model: violated with the first finding as counterexample, verified when every listed rule had something to check and found nothing, inconclusive when a listed rule had nothing to check",
        "Run every declared negative of a trace claim (its scenario with the scenario's own faults plus the negative's) and report the claim verified only when it holds on its scenarios and each negative, at least one, violates it; a trace claim with no negative, or with a negative that does not definitely violate it, is inconclusive and the explanation says which",
        "Evaluate refines claims (src/core/refinement.js): in the trace of each scenario that runs the implementation behavior, check that its initial configurations and every transition it fires map through its refinement mapping to the transition its model would fire (from, to, event, guard true and first in declaration order, effects giving the mapped post-variables, the same emitted events) or to a stutter that changes no mapped state or variable and emits nothing; violated at the first failing step with the steps up to it as a trace and a reason; then explore every execution of the scenario's input that delivers simultaneous entries in another order (src/core/refinement-explore.js, with the step of src/core/replay.js) and check every step it reaches, violated with the delivery order as part of the counterexample; inconclusive when the exploration reaches its configuration cap or the step limit or meets what it does not enumerate (faults, draws, cancellations, instances), when a run ends in an error, a model expression reads what the mapping does not define, or no step matched a model transition",
        "In refines claims, tell a transition's queued entries apart into emits and timers by its effects, compare timers only with a model transition that sets timers, and read other behaviors named by model expressions from the same trace configuration, through the machine that refines them when they do not run themselves"
      ],
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "claims-evaluate-in",
          "name": "Evaluate claims",
          "direction": "provides",
          "contract": "evaluate-claims",
          "protocol": "in-process call",
          "description": "Evaluate every claim or one claim of a model."
        }
      ],
      "unknowns": [
        "Behavioral results hold for the listed scenario traces only; there is no exhaustive state-space exploration and no probabilistic evaluation.",
        "Claim evaluation runs on the caller's thread. In the application session.evaluate runs it on the main thread inside every semantic commit, and trace and metric claims run engine scenarios there (src/core/claims.js), unlike the Simulation tab, which runs its scenario in the engine worker. The cost on large models is not measured (WG-4775, finding F06).",
        "Refines claims are checked on the deterministic trace of each scenario with its own faults, not over every delivery order and fault subset the explorer enumerates: the explorer returns state-invariant verdicts, not steps (WG-4852).",
        "The tree stamp is coarse: any edit of a stamped file makes every evidence result stale until verify runs again, not only the results that depend on it.",
        "The application evaluates claims without current stamps, so every recorded fact is unverifiable there and the claims resting on them read undecided in the application (WG-5688).",
        "A lean-theorem claim is not bound to what its statement says: a theorem that mentions the named code but states nothing about it is still proved; a change of its statement is refused by admit unless a superseding implication exists, but the claim does not pin the statement it expects (WG-5690)."
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "src/core/claims.js",
          "src/core/concordance.js",
          "src/core/refinement.js",
          "src/core/refinement-explore.js"
        ],
        "language": "js"
      },
      "tags": [
        "verdict-kernel"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "contract-conformance",
      "name": "Contract conformance",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "Lets an event be a request, a response or an error of a contract at a boundary, so that a simulated trace is checked and reads like a request log. Holds the type-string parser shared by the static and the trace checks.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Parse the type strings of payload fields and contract inputs and outputs (string, number, boolean, null, literals, unions, arrays); a type it cannot read is opaque and every value fits it",
        "Derive from a contract the fields and error codes it allows for a request, a response and an error",
        "Check a trace: for each step whose event is a contract event, the payload values fit the declared types, a request carries every input that is not optional, an error code is among the contract's errors, and a response or error answers an earlier unanswered request of the same contract (matched by the correlation field when the event declares one, otherwise by count); each violation is a failed automatic check carrying the step",
        "Render a trace as request-log lines (src/core/contract-trace.js, requestLog): the contract's binding (method and path, or operation) or else the event name for a request, 'ok' for a response and 'refused <code>' for an error; steps that are not contract events give no line",
        "Read traces and models only: the engine, its Lean semantics and the engine input format are untouched"
      ],
      "unknowns": [
        "Object and generic types (for example {text, hash} or Operation[]) are opaque to the type check: their values are accepted unchecked.",
        "A response is paired with its request by one correlation field or by count; there is no timeout or ordering rule for unanswered requests at the end of a run."
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "src/core/type-expr.js",
          "src/core/contract-trace.js"
        ],
        "language": "js"
      },
      "tags": [
        "verdict-kernel"
      ]
    },
    {
