    {
      "id": "semantic-abstraction",
      "name": "Semantic abstraction engine",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "Derives the architectural model at any cut of any hierarchy from the relationships: blocks, lifted interactions with complete witness sets and homomorphic summaries, fibres, boundaries, derived interfaces checked against declared ports, connector contractions, contract realisation paths, realisation maps between hierarchies and the diagnostics of the abstraction; and says which derived facts and claim results an edit can change.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Lift the relationships through a cut's abstraction map per kind or coarsened kind, each derived interaction carrying every relationship it stands for (sorted) and a summary that is a monoid homomorphism: count, kinds, contracts, carried data, delivery guarantees, provenance mix and ports",
        "Keep each block's fibre (its internal relationships), boundary and derived interface, and compare the interface with the ports the block declares (encapsulation and realisation); an opaque block's declared interface is authoritative",
        "Attach the facts of an element a cut opens to that element as an implicit block, so every relationship lies in exactly one fibre or witness set",
        "Check relationships that end at ports (requires to provides, one contract), declared coarse facts at transparent composites (witnessed, kind preserved), and realisation maps between hierarchies",
        "Report the realisation path of every contract hop by hop, flagging hops whose events carry no contract",
        "Check its own invariants on a model: witness completeness, boundary accounting, recoverability, interfaces from boundaries, functoriality, kind preservation and determinism",
        "Record footprints on derived facts and claim results, and re-derive after an edit only the facts whose footprint the edit touches",
        "Read extracted and observed facts as data (src/core/extraction.js) and keep them beside the declared relationships with their own provenance: one imports relationship per pair of module elements the extracted imports connect, so that an extracted import is a witness of the derived interaction at every cut; reconcile the declarations with them (a declared static dependency inside the surface is redundant; a declared runtime interaction no recording shows is not observed, never false); report what a check rests on (declared, extracted or observed)",
        "Derive the message interactions of the behaviors (an event emitted by a behavior of one element and consumed by a behavior of another) and check them against the relationships: every cross-element message witnessed at the base and, through the cut, by a derived interaction made by a relationship between the same two elements, never by a different witness (V5); and the events that cross a relationship that declares a contract typed by that contract (V7)"
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "src/core/abstraction.js",
          "src/core/footprints.js",
          "src/core/extraction.js",
          "src/core/function-composition.js",
          "src/core/fault-lifting.js",
          "src/core/coherence.js"
        ],
        "language": "js",
        "notes": "Pure functions of a model snapshot, memoised per snapshot and cut. The lifting rule is a small function rather than an integrity relation: the rule language ranges over entities, and blocks, module elements and witness sets are not entities."
      },
      "unknowns": [
        "Integrity rules cannot read derived interactions yet: the rule language quantifies over entities and stores no witnesses, so the derived tuples are read by claims, views and the command line only (WG-5709).",
        "The invariants (lift functoriality with coarsening, absence transfer, boundary characterisation, reachability and acyclicity transfer, recoverability, incremental equals full re-derivation) are checked by property tests and by the abstraction-invariants claims on this model, not proved in Lean (WG-5710).",
        "Level-bound claim verdicts are decided here in JavaScript, outside the verified kernel of tag verdict-kernel; whether the derivation joins that kernel is part of WG-4785's scope."
      ]
    },
